From a brand new Ubuntu server on DO, with the Rails image

* Run locally: bundle exec cap production deploy:initial  This starts to set up the app.
* ssh to server
* sudo su rails
* cd /home/rails/apps/brick_city_depot
* vim shared/config/master.key (copy in the production key)
* vim shared/config/credentials/production.key (copy in the production key)
* echo "gem: --no-document" > ~/.gemrc
* gem install bundler
* chown -R rails:rails /home/rails/apps/brick_city_depot
* echo "alias bcd='cd /home/rails/apps/brick_city_depot'" >> ~/.bashrc && source ~/.bashrc
* Often we can run OOM during the bundle install. This will throw an error like this:
  SSHKit::Runner::ExecuteError: Exception while executing on host x.x.x.x: bundle exit status: 137 (SSHKit::Runner::ExecuteError)
  Add some swap memory so the bundle install can run: https://www.digitalocean.com/community/tutorials/how-to-add-swap-space-on-ubuntu-22-04
* (As the root user) sudo adduser rails sudo (I don't want to do this, but Capistrano is
  running sudo on commands I didn't ask it to, and failing since rails wasn't in the sudoers.)

SETTING UP THE DB:
* exit (to get to the root user)
* sudo -u postgres psql
* (in psql) create database brickcity_production;
* \q (to exit out of psql)

WEB SERVER:
* As the root user:
  edit /etc/systemd/system/rails.service and change the WorkingDirectory and ExecStart to include the RAILS_ENV and RAILS_MASTER_KEY
  systemctl daemon-reload
  edit /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/rails and change the root (Make sure to include /current/) Also, change localhost to 127.0.0.1
* (As the rails user) vim ~/.bashrc
  * Add `RAILS_ENV=production`
* source ~/.bashrc
* vim /etc/sudoers.d/rails and add the following:
  rails ALL=NOPASSWD: /bin/systemctl restart sidekiq.service, /bin/systemctl status sidekiq.service, /bin/systemctl restart rails.service, /bin/systemctl status rails.service, /bin/systemctl restart rails
* (From the Rails root) mkdir -p shared/tmp/pids




